WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:08.660
- Good morning, everybody.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.660
I think we are all set to begin.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.640
Thanks for coming this
morning to the third

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.878
and final event of this
year's Tanner Lectures

00:00:14.878 --> 00:00:16.800
roundtable discussion.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.410
It's been a wonderful event so far

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.750
and Achille Mbembe in the middle,

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.540
research professor from the
University of the Witwatersrand

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.230
in Johannesburg needs no further
introduction at this point.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.070
Joining him this morning are two treasures

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.610
from the Yale faculty.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.882
Inderpal Grewal is a professor affiliated

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:34.640
with several programs.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.350
Women's Gender and Sexuality
Studies where she is chair.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.810
You're still chair there?

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:40.643
- Yes.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.063
- American studies, Ethnicity,
Race and Migration studies

00:00:46.063 --> 00:00:48.380
and the South Asian Studies Council.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.153
I think I've hit them all
but if there are more.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.470
Her latest book out just a few
months ago in fact from Duke

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.100
is Saving the Security State:

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.220
Exceptional Citizens in
Twenty-First-Century America.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.030
Michael Denning is the
William R. Kenan Jr. professor

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.440
and likewise, multiply
affiliated with American Studies,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.780
English and Ethnicity,
Race and Migration Studies.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.210
He also co-directs the initiative

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.170
on labor and culture at Yale.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.070
His latest book from Verso
about a few years ago

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.380
is Noise Uprising: The Audiopolitics

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.323
of a World Musical Revolution.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.030
A word about how we'll
proceed this morning.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.620
Professors Grewal and Denning
will each speak briefly

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.050
opening out some questions and issues

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.340
from the lectures you've heard
over the last two afternoons.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.590
Professor Mbembe will respond
and our three panelists

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:39.880
will then shape the
conversation as they wish.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.130
After 50 minutes or so,
we'll open the conversation

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.550
to questions from the
floor and after that,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.830
we'll adjourn to the
reception room straight down

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.540
the hall behind you for a light
lunch and more conversation.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.990
Thank you all for being here.

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:56.249
(attendees applauding)

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.249
- Thank you.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.204
I guess I begin.

00:02:00.204 --> 00:02:01.200
- [Achille] Okay.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.020
- So first to thank Gary
for making this happen,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.490
for Achille for being here and for giving

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.950
two fantastic lectures that resonated

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.800
with some of the key questions of the day

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.100
and some of the key theoretical
issues that we grapple with

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.620
in studying race,
migration, post-coloniality.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.620
So what you said, Achille, spoke very much

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.370
to many of our key
concerns, not only topical

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.393
but theoretical research wise as well.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.320
And in the second lecture, I
think one of the key issues

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.270
that you brought up was
the question of migration

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.830
and the movement of Africans around Europe

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.700
and North America and what is
called the crisis of migration

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.310
but rather is a kind of scandal
and a crime on migration,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.450
one might say in Europe as well.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.273
So some of those questions
really spoke very loudly to us.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.070
And you began in the first
lecture with talking about

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.600
a very different kind
of paradigm of mobility

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.620
that many of us have been thinking about

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:22.620
and trying to grapple with
not just in thinking about

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.660
the present and the ways
in which boundaries, walls

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.800
and the bounds of the
nation-state, forms of power

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.730
are not enclosures but
are permeable, right?

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.920
That they're moving, they're mobile,

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.560
we're grappling with the kind of mobility

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.170
not just off capital of goods as you said

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.260
but also of persons.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.580
But we're also grappling with the mobility

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.077
of things that we thought
were stable, right?

00:03:56.077 --> 00:03:59.440
The kind of concepts of
bounded nation-states,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.410
the idea of sovereignty, the
idea of Westphalian state,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:07.730
all of these that you
brought out so importantly.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.390
And one of the issues
that comes out of that

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.950
is you what you also spoke
about in the first lecture

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:14.750
which is a question of sovereignty.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.480
Where does sovereignty lie?

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.420
How does sovereignty move?

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.240
How does it get, you know, you referred

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.633
to Saskia Sassen's talking
about, unbundled sovereignty.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:29.700
And the question there
that I would ask of you

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.880
is looking back to the work
of Thomas Hanson and others

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.040
that who argues that
the kind of unbundling

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.840
and dispersal of sovereignty is not new,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.140
that it was a condition
of colonialism as well.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.750
So one of the issues that
I would want to ask you

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.020
to say more about is the question

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.440
of not just about the
unbundling of sovereignty

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.503
but the paradigm of nobility.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.180
Is it new?

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.260
Should we have thought
about it as a question

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:06.570
of a changing formation of nation-state

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.290
or do we think about it in
terms of a paradigm shift

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:14.633
in which we have always not addressed

00:05:14.633 --> 00:05:18.230
the mobility of people, right?

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.920
That mobility is something
that has always been there

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.380
but the kind of emergence
of the Westphalian state

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.730
and all of these other bounded
enclosure making processes

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:32.560
and disciplinarity have
not allowed us to see it

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.010
in some ways and that
we're grappling with it.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.180
So that's one of the questions

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.690
that would be really helpful for us,

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:44.150
not just in thinking about the politics

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.810
but the organization of knowledge as well

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.580
in the university for instance.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.580
About disciplinarity the area
states and national boundaries

00:05:54.257 --> 00:05:59.100
as enclosures of thought
as well of knowledge.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.693
So some of that would be,

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.210
that's one question that I would ask.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.700
A second question I think is
something that you brought up

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.720
also really importantly that
I've been grappling with it

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.710
about security regimes.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.583
That securitization of borders of people

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:22.970
is sort of this condition of the present

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.530
where development has been securitized,

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.610
everything is becoming securitized now

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:31.910
and you you spoke about that as well.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.460
But one of the complex
things about security

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.400
is that I think there's an affective

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.590
notion of security as
well where people desire

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:46.070
some kind of settlement,
where people desire a care,

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:49.840
welfare, in particular, kinds of spaces.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.840
And when you spoke of the
kind of utopian thought

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.910
of mobility of people being
able to move everywhere,

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.500
one of the things we'd come up again,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.820
the question yesterday that
Alicia was also asking,

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.410
what do we do with people
who want to settle,

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.750
who wants something from a
state, who want documents,

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.230
who want social security, who won't care,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.310
who want all of these things?

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:17.743
So that would be another
question to think about.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:22.450
A third would be the
question of technology.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:27.450
How do we think of surveillance
and technology as again,

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.000
not just something a new kind of project

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.900
but rather that technology produces fears

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.290
and is always harness toward certain

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.260
ideological end as well.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.800
So how is what you talked about

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.651
as the cellular and fragmented
way in which surveillance

00:07:48.651 --> 00:07:51.773
and technology are produced,

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.893
sort of part of a longer history, right?

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.640
The kind of fingerprints
are a colonial apparatus.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.610
So we have these for a really long time.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.980
So how do we think of those as well

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.670
as being part of a history of colonialism

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.670
that you have shed so much light on

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:17.820
and helped us understand in so many ways.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.820
So I guess that some of
those would be key questions

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.700
to think about in terms of habitation

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:30.700
abode the question of
settling, the question

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.820
of people wanting to be in certain places.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.430
And my last point would
the kind of question

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:42.803
of the verticality of
power that you spoke of.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:46.420
Weitzman and others have talked about

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:49.670
the way we need to think of
power not just as horizontal

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.550
or lateral but as from
the sky down, right?

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:55.270
That satellites and technologies

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.270
allow us to think of cities and places

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.300
in a whole different
kind of optic as well.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:07.300
And of course, he uses it in
terms of kind of diagnosing

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.250
human rights abuses and power.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.300
But I wonder how you would think

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.950
about that verticality of power
in relation to technology,

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.500
in relation to the kind

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.500
of post-colonial project
of migration and enclosure

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:28.240
that you were also trying
to, you know, grappling with

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.773
and questioning in all kinds of ways.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.067
So I would put out those
sort of four questions.

00:09:38.675 --> 00:09:43.675
- Okay, first of all, I
would like to thank Inderpal

00:09:43.742 --> 00:09:48.742
for having been here,
having attended the lectures

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:54.380
and having so attentively and so carefully

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:59.380
drawn out the four pragmatics
she has just shared with us,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:06.190
all of which I think extremely
useful for any discussion

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:11.683
on question of mobility in our times.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:18.470
I think you are absolutely
right to insist on the necessity

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:21.873
for grounding all of this

00:10:21.873 --> 00:10:25.190
in deeper history

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.493
or deeper histories for that matter.

00:10:33.796 --> 00:10:37.057
A condition I agree for eliciting

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:42.460
possibly what what might be new

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:47.033
in all that we are seeing right now.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.050
A longer history of technologies,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.683
longer history of forms of power,

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.623
longer history of,

00:10:59.417 --> 00:11:00.930
of questions you raise

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.468
such as national boundaries, enclosures,

00:11:04.468 --> 00:11:09.468
modes of thought and forms
of production of knowledge.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.680
So I'll try to not really respond but just

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.823
rebound on what it is
that I've heard from you.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:30.190
I don't think the novelty is
about technologies as such,

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:33.260
talking about technologies in particular.

00:11:39.095 --> 00:11:41.012
The novelty stems from,

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:49.450
I would argue, a face of
the history of capitalism

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:54.113
many have characterized as
a phase of acceleration.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:01.240
The novelty lies in the fact

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:06.240
of our having domesticated speed

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.773
at a level and seen before.

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.890
I really believe that
this question of speed,

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:19.070
of velocity is what is,

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.723
if there is anything new, that is it.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:27.050
I was referring his day to
the work of Paul Virilio

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:31.323
in particular since he is one
of those who have helped us,

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:37.530
some extent, to understand
the centrality of speed

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:43.067
in the making and remaking of our world.

00:12:45.705 --> 00:12:48.055
Domestication of speed
as it is related to the,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:58.400
in particular this question of light.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:03.280
I mentioned yesterday almost in passing,

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.420
I think I should have been more on that

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:14.510
because a number of technologies

00:13:14.895 --> 00:13:19.895
adduced to war, warfare,

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:25.730
security for that matter but
also the production of wealth

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.113
in this age of financial capitalism.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.823
Most of them, indeed,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:41.200
maximizing, forms of
maximization of speed.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.100
They rely on speed.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.620
The new forms of capitalism rely on speed.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.660
I refer to algorithmic trading.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:53.830
There a number of new studies
of those forms of exchange

00:13:56.210 --> 00:14:01.210
that show indeed extent
to which they in fact

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.810
rely as resource of speed.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.503
So speed seems to me to be the novelty.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:15.370
And the organizing principle
for their forms of life,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.840
we were referring to.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:26.840
Now, if indeed speed is
becoming the resource

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.803
for the remaking of our world,

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:37.240
it goes without saying that the,

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:41.763
that the ultimate forms of sovereignty,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:47.220
the most efficient ways
of exercising power

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:54.080
have to do with how to capture speed

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:59.780
and how to redistribute
speed in an equal ways.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.200
So those who

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:08.400
must go slow if you want
to put it like that.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:13.130
And who are those who
might not be able to move?

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:18.130
Who are those who might
be able to move at a speed

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.990
with a velocity that is unequally?

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:30.990
It seems to me that's how we
have to frame these questions

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:34.593
in this age.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.200
As far as the organization
of knowledge is concerned,

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:45.070
and a lot of our knowledge
in the humanities,

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:50.730
in the social sciences and
probably in other fields

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.340
is predicated on sedentarity.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:57.883
It is predicated on
people settled somewhere,

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.500
moving of course back
but moving from within

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.430
a space that is delimitated

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:14.170
as fundamentally in
enclosed locations, places

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.750
so forth and so on.

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:20.290
So our knowledge mirrors somewhat

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:23.510
this partition of the world

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:31.440
that is predicated on sedentarity.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:38.070
And if indeed we live in an era of,

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:42.023
as I was suggesting is the
planetary entanglement,

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.990
obviously, this requires different forms

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:48.640
of production of knowledge.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.743
Itinerant knowledge for instance.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:58.440
It requires new imagination
of institutions, university,

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:04.963
that wouldn't have walls for instance.

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:09.383
University beyond walls,
knowledge beyond walls.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:17.230
And these experiments are already being...

00:17:19.237 --> 00:17:22.480
There are places where such
experimentations are going on

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.020
where people experimenting
with forms of knowledge

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:29.970
that would be bounded either
nationally originally,

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.183
forms of knowledge that would circulate,

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:37.713
knowledge is in circulation.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:40.860
It seems to me that is the challenge

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.493
this kind of analysis poses.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:49.453
One last thing verticality of power,

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:54.783
Isa Habreder Iali's work.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:01.640
And in fact Hazel raised
the same issue informally

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:04.860
the other night at a dinner.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:09.703
What about the vertical that
which comes from the sky,

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.390
how do we take into consideration

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:17.393
not only the sea or the
desert but also the air?

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:23.350
And I think it's a very important question

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:27.140
but what strikes me is that in fact

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:32.570
is the fact that it's
the every articulations

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:38.873
of all these optical points of view,

00:18:38.873 --> 00:18:43.750
it's not the privileging
of the sky as such.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:48.513
No, is it the privileges of
terrestrial or the maritime.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:53.475
The process of conversion of the maritime

00:18:53.475 --> 00:18:57.392
into the terrestrial,
the terrestrial into sky,

00:18:59.298 --> 00:19:03.700
it's that circulation between
different optical points

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:06.183
of view which seems to me to be going on.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.040
And which seems to me to be producing

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:14.750
an entirely new paradigm
of power, sovereignty

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:15.763
and domination.

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:21.720
And the transformation of all
of this into codes and data.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.270
Including the transformation
of the human itself

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.563
into a code and into a data.

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:31.793
And therefore the,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:40.280
the distance, let's say
they're leaving behind us

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:43.400
of all moral the human with which

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.780
the humanities have been working.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:51.410
The human appended to to an object.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.060
The human appended to an animal,

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.110
the human appended to
different other species beings

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.110
and all that captured into codes and data,

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:07.433
that is what seems to
me to be the novelty.

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:12.803
So long and torturous
answer to your question.

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:15.273
- So can I press you in one question?

00:20:15.273 --> 00:20:16.106
- Yes, yes.

00:20:16.106 --> 00:20:18.170
- Which is that some people,

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:21.080
some thinkers are talking about in the,

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:24.980
you mention of algorithms
that is now sovereign

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.180
is what is sovereign is data.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.260
That we're now thinking about
an unbundling of sovereignty.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:33.700
We have seized that kind
of sovereignty of data

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:34.670
but on the other hand--

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:35.503
- The sovereignty is one who--

00:20:35.503 --> 00:20:37.437
- The kind of racial algorithm argument.

00:20:37.437 --> 00:20:38.340
- Definitely.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:42.760
- Also suggests that data
is not that sovereign

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:44.620
all on its own, that that's informed

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:45.453
by the very forces that--

00:20:45.453 --> 00:20:47.970
- It is, it is absolutely informed.

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:52.970
It is shaped and reshaped
by various other factors.

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:56.740
Including race, gender,
class and the rest.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:59.943
It's not neutral nor is it objective.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:02.783
It's not at all objective.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.490
- I could continue but let me ask

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:11.870
a couple of other questions

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.430
which I think are actually
from the other side

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:18.520
of your account of
geopolitics and biopolitics.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:21.420
You gave us powerful, I wanna thank you

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:25.250
for the powerful account of
the mobile and portable border,

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:27.650
the way border work and border processing

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.930
and border enforcement can
move anywhere in some ways

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.690
and is now in the
interstices of daily life

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:36.350
and it made me think of how
across the traffic routes

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:40.490
in particular where mobile beings move,

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:43.460
not only airports with
their various security lines

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.860
which were so aware but
I was thinking indeed

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.610
of roads and highways
with their speed cameras.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.870
They're easy passes, the rules of the road

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:54.150
that have actually made driver's license

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:57.390
is such an important issue
for undocumented workers

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:59.420
and for migrants in that kind of way.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:01.240
And I also actually wanna thank you

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:03.270
for that powerful assertion

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:07.520
of the right of all mobile
mammals to move around the Earth,

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.960
to extend as you said to the
poor members of the Earth

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:14.670
that taken for granted
rights of the US passport.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:18.230
But I wonder whether the issue of borders

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:22.223
and the question of movement
are really the same question.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:24.820
Because it's as you yourself put it,

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.410
the business of a border to be crossed

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:30.350
and movement might be nothing more

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.190
than the crossing of borders.

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.340
I was thinking this morning as I crossed

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:38.340
from the town of Guildford
into the city of New Haven.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.270
That moment when we crossed the border

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:41.880
from Connecticut in New York

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.550
and see the looming towers of Manhattan

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.694
and that sort of excitement.

00:22:46.694 --> 00:22:51.694
And whether or not borders
are really about movement

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:55.050
or the issue of free movement

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:57.780
then as you also said at one point

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:01.090
about the ways of classifying people.

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:04.590
And if what you mean by a borderless world

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:09.270
is not a world of free
movement but a world of not,

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.573
where people are not classified.

00:23:12.806 --> 00:23:15.470
And that might be a
different way to think this.

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:19.440
So let me ask two
questions following that.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.710
One on the biopolitics and
geopolitics of sovereignty

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:27.150
which you raised and one on one might call

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:32.050
the bioeconomics and
geoeconomics of accumulation.

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:34.730
So the first, I'm kind
of intrigued to hear more

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:39.730
about the implications
of Ghana's right to abode

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:41.540
as a kind of refiguring.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.790
What what are the
philosophical implications

00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:46.210
of that refiguring of sovereignty

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:49.490
which from my very quick study

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:52.660
seems to involve two different aspects.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:57.010
One is the right of Ghanaian's
to move freely in Ghana,

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:59.190
the right to leave and enter Ghana,

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:01.760
immunity from expulsion from Ghana,

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:05.060
exactly the right that i
had to drive from Guildford

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:08.390
to New York or to California in that way.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:10.880
But secondly and more remarkably,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.150
the extension of that
right in the Act of 2000

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:19.150
as I understand it to a
person of African descent

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:22.390
in the diaspora, that's the quote.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:25.130
And so this a right that includes

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:27.670
the right to remain indefinitely,

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:30.060
the right to work without a work permit,

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:32.170
the right to come without a visa,

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:34.050
the right to have children and dependents

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:35.720
covered by that same abode.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:40.070
And so I guess my question
is do you raise this

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:44.770
as fundamentally a part of
a politics of reparations.

00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:48.950
Is this really a sense of
repairing the forced confinement,

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:53.760
the chained migration of
the Atlantic slave trade,

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:57.100
the divisions of the continent
you talked about in Berlin

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:01.210
or is this a sense that the full freedom

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:04.170
of movement of mammal, human mammals

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:08.510
depends perhaps less on an
abstract call for open borders

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:11.890
than on the repair of
specific migratory circuits,

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:16.230
historical circuits on the
reforging of the lengths

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:21.113
of what our president has
denounced as chain migration.

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:26.390
And the second question, so
that's all one question I guess.

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:30.270
You powerfully begin both
days with the question

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:32.310
to whom does the Earth belong

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:36.340
and who determines its
partition and distribution.

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:40.020
And yet your answers
focused, vitally, no doubt,

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:44.290
on the borders that were
maintained by sovereign states.

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:47.890
But doesn't the question of
the partition of the Earth

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:52.930
also depend as much on
the partition of the Earth

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:56.100
as different capital, as private property

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:59.150
owned by transnational corporations

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:01.680
with no trespassing signs there,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.860
border work that's done by
an army of security guards,

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:09.207
often staffed by those mobile migrants

00:26:09.207 --> 00:26:11.290
and they disproportionately

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:14.170
with the temporary issuing
of permits and passes

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:18.620
to get on to those spaces as
well as the gated communities

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:20.250
of private homeowners.

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:23.540
Again, both of those in
some ways outside the state,

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.860
again serviced by the labor of migrants

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:31.840
but who are off-limits to those ones.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:36.250
And I guess, so I would
wonder how does one imagine

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.580
and this is really to
return to Alicia's question

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:43.200
which I won't try to repeat but
I'll actually just quote her

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.530
from a written piece because I couldn't,

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:49.220
but how do we imagine a
new mobility of humans

00:26:49.220 --> 00:26:52.600
that isn't simply the flexibility of labor

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:55.980
for the movement of
capital but a new kind of,

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:57.630
in here's where I'm quoting her,

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.670
a new kind of migrant justice
that would allow migrants

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.700
to achieve and exercise social power

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:08.670
without obtaining formal citizenship

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:12.760
where migrants would be
binational civic actors,

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:17.022
whether some kind of space
between not being a citizen

00:27:17.022 --> 00:27:20.450
or being a citizen and whether that raises

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.300
and I guess the final question then

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:26.440
is partly you quite provocatively

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:30.000
suggested that mobility was the motor

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.720
of social and economic change

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:37.523
and not actually internal
struggles inside a society.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:41.560
And I was intrigued at
whether having thought so long

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:45.280
and hard about changes in
20th century South Africa

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:48.033
and across the South African continent.

00:27:48.890 --> 00:27:50.110
Is it true?

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:53.270
What were the forms of mobility

00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:56.370
that actually caused those
social transformations

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.290
and economic transformations?

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:03.290
So as to see how migrants
might in the 21st century

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:07.690
so reshape the world.

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:10.483
- Yes, okay.

00:28:14.143 --> 00:28:18.000
So all very interesting
comments and questions.

00:28:23.489 --> 00:28:27.989
Let us speak first about the
right of abode since you,

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:32.970
you picked it up as a key element

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:35.223
of yesterday's presentation.

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:43.690
I don't think that in the
Ghanaian understanding,

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:48.690
it is conceived as being
part of the fourth freedom

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:53.363
we spoke about yesterday.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:56.153
It's not,

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:02.203
at least not immediately thought of in,

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:05.613
in those terms.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:13.130
In terms of Ghana or
Africa for that matter,

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:17.550
being able to institute

00:29:23.692 --> 00:29:27.967
a regime of flexible labor,
market labor circulation

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:31.050
and so forth and so on.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:34.550
That is taken care of by the treaty

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.910
that has just been signed in Kigali

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:40.050
which I referred to yesterday.

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:44.980
The single market treaty which would allow

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:49.980
possibly the movement
of labor from places,

00:29:55.910 --> 00:30:00.063
production to the rest.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:06.160
So the right to abode now
encompasses a number of aspects.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:11.903
It is,

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:22.660
its aim is to make sure as you indicated

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:30.300
that Ghanaians and Africans in general

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.100
or people of African descent

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:40.310
take ownership of this
specific space called Africa.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:46.440
Ghana being one instanciation
of that larger space.

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:55.800
Which would mean they
have the right to come in,

00:30:55.980 --> 00:31:00.980
they have the right to settle,
they have the right to work,

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:05.631
they have the right to be taken care of

00:31:05.631 --> 00:31:10.631
insofar as the state
attends to matters of care.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:19.333
And they have the right to come and go.

00:31:25.010 --> 00:31:29.430
But the right of abode is also

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:34.230
understood as a specific right

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:38.250
extended to people of African origin

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:43.250
not for as a result of
historical circumstances

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:49.280
have happened to be located elsewhere.

00:31:53.250 --> 00:31:56.540
It is similar in that sense
to the right of return

00:31:57.590 --> 00:32:02.590
Israel has instituted for
Jewish people, Jewish diaspora

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:09.340
and you're absolutely
right to suggest that

00:32:10.459 --> 00:32:13.053
there is a reparative element in it.

00:32:15.109 --> 00:32:18.480
And an implicit recognition

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:22.370
of the responsibility of African powers

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:26.540
in the whole business of
the Atlantic slave trade

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:27.373
in particular.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:33.670
But these reparative element is also

00:32:33.670 --> 00:32:35.383
has to do with self reparation.

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:42.730
And in fact, in the juridical debates

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:46.470
that preceded the
adoption of this specific

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:48.073
article of the constitution,

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:55.163
that reparative dimension
was explicitly spoken about.

00:32:58.040 --> 00:32:59.033
So,

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:03.520
it seems to me that

00:33:09.250 --> 00:33:11.770
the concept of the right to abode

00:33:14.010 --> 00:33:18.410
is somewhat of a response
in the part to the question

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:22.410
you were asking, saying
some people want to settle.

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:27.410
Some people want to to be taken care of.

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:33.690
So it's part of that.

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:37.860
It's part of an attempt at making sure

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:42.860
that people of African
descent or African people

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:49.530
find on this Earth that
presumably belongs to all,

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:54.200
a place where they do not have to justify

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:57.630
who they are, why they are there

00:33:57.630 --> 00:33:59.193
and what they are doing there.

00:34:03.250 --> 00:34:06.680
Where to be black is no longer liability

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:11.360
because they doesn't
seem to be such a place

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:16.210
in our world right now
away you are not forced

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:18.820
to justify why you are black.

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:20.880
So the right of abode would be

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:26.930
that mechanism that allows you
to not have to be classified

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:34.263
and to not have to justify
why you are who you are.

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:40.900
That's how I would expand
the concept of reparation

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:46.583
when applied to these
specific type of right.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.600
You also right to suggest
that there are various

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:59.280
modalities of partition of the Earth.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:04.280
We have the Westphalian
model where states apportion

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:10.960
parts of the Earth and
distributed among themselves

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:14.083
but there are other modalities that are,

00:35:14.994 --> 00:35:16.280
I'm not suggesting the esteem

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.340
but maybe not strongly enough

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:22.070
operated by the works of capital.

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:24.513
In a place like such as Johannesburg,

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:32.310
in the aftermath of a
parting urban planning

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:37.110
by governmental agencies

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:41.173
hardly operates any law.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.830
The city is rebuilt basically by

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:52.023
private agencies.

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:58.560
They decide where they
want to create new suburbs,

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:02.020
they build roads, they give names

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:05.053
to the boulevards and the avenues.

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:10.040
And in buildings as new suburbs.

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:15.040
They reproduce the
mechanisms of segregation

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:20.520
or they ratify

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:26.480
the modes of redistribution of urban space

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:29.660
which used to operate under
the parts of the world.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:32.915
So it's no longer just
the property of the state,

00:36:32.915 --> 00:36:36.690
capital is very much involved in

00:36:39.940 --> 00:36:44.823
making, remaking and making borders.

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:48.620
What forms of mobility might cause

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:50.793
what types of transformations?

00:36:53.248 --> 00:36:57.143
This is a interesting
sociological question.

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.233
In the South African case,

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:10.490
in fact, the collapse of a party,

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:14.640
it was the result of people

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:17.740
mobilizing of course

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:24.880
via political activities
but it was also the result

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:30.610
of the collapse of the past system.

00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:34.820
The past system was a
system whereby South Africa

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:38.920
was carved into internal borders

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:45.910
because blacks were not citizens
of the South African state

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:51.060
because they were citizens
of the Bantustans.

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:55.460
They needed permits to come and work

00:37:56.550 --> 00:38:00.423
in those places where
they could find jobs.

00:38:02.290 --> 00:38:05.130
They were not city residents as such

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:09.130
at a certain point during the day,

00:38:09.130 --> 00:38:11.340
I think five o'clock or 5:30,

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:13.350
they were supposed to leave the city

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:15.980
and go and reside in specific places

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:18.283
that were allocated especially for them.

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:22.070
Their presence in the city necessitated

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:24.253
the possession of a pass.

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:28.840
So what happens by the mid-80s

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:33.380
is massive campaign of disobedience

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:37.280
where people decide they are
no longer going to carry passes

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:40.450
and you have an influx,

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:44.380
the influx control laws collapse entirely.

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:46.630
Influx control laws were,

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:51.000
the laws regulating internal migrations

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:53.350
within South Africa itself,

00:38:53.350 --> 00:38:57.960
laws which way racially
of course configured.

00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:01.980
It collapses, people go into cities

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:04.370
in Johannesburg for instance,

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:08.520
the most cosmopolitan
neighborhood Hillbrow

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:13.080
which was peopled inhabited
early on especially by

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:20.920
people from the Jewish diaspora.

00:39:20.920 --> 00:39:24.420
It's overwhelmed by the arrival

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:27.210
of those who are not supposed to be there.

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:31.340
That's how a party becomes
partly and government

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:34.863
in addition goes to the manipulation of,

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:40.520
unleashing of insurrectionary violence.

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:43.220
So we see in this instance how it is that

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:49.580
the capacity for people to move

00:39:52.370 --> 00:39:56.623
changes the urban configuration,

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:01.810
it changes completely the ways

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:04.120
in which the apparatus of capture

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:09.120
which is the state operates
and that leads to change.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.390
Let's say social transformations.

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:17.040
But I'm sure we could extend
this example to others as well

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:17.933
in the continent.

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:23.620
So just to finish on that,
I think that there is a...

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:27.337
Migrants have...

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:38.530
Bearers of specific
transformation capacities,

00:40:39.480 --> 00:40:44.480
we haven't examined seriously.

00:40:46.690 --> 00:40:48.620
Migrants is probably

00:40:51.240 --> 00:40:55.423
one of those resources,

00:40:56.960 --> 00:40:59.800
those who would like to
change the world today

00:40:59.800 --> 00:41:03.887
should invest in eliciting and unleashing.

00:41:08.270 --> 00:41:09.170
How?

00:41:09.170 --> 00:41:11.763
That's the topic for
the next Tanner Lecture.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:17.540
- I guess we should open it up to...

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:26.900
Okay, so if I could press you a little bit

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:28.630
on the last question.

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:32.440
I think Michael read the quote from Alicia

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:35.800
which sort of suggests a different kind,

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:40.560
a kind of moving the migrant
away from capture by labor

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:43.233
and capital to something else.

00:41:44.260 --> 00:41:48.350
And I'm wondering about the kind of ways

00:41:48.350 --> 00:41:52.070
in which European modernity took mobility

00:41:52.070 --> 00:41:57.070
as liberatory because it
came from modernist notions

00:41:58.170 --> 00:42:02.513
of exile, itinerants,
adventure, all of those things.

00:42:04.300 --> 00:42:07.380
So one of the shadows if one might say

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:11.110
of mobility is that
sort of colonial history

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:13.720
of mobility as well, right?

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.200
And so the struggle it seems to me

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:18.820
in what Alicia is saying, Michael's

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:23.820
is how to take that shadow
away from migration,

00:42:24.950 --> 00:42:28.900
one hand of capital, one
hand of sort of a link

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:32.763
to European kind of project of modernity

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:39.720
to somewhere else and what
that might be is indeed utopian

00:42:41.240 --> 00:42:43.250
but how to imagine that utopia

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:46.823
is I think the kind of struggle
that we're dealing with.

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:52.270
Right and what constitutes mobility

00:42:52.270 --> 00:42:55.760
as the engine of that
is the other question.

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:58.130
- I think what one would have to take

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:02.300
some very specific cases to answer this.

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.660
As I was suggesting yesterday,

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:08.623
in pre-colonial Africa,
mobility was the norm.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:12.790
It was really the norm.

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:17.790
In some societies what was
not normal was the sedentary.

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:23.060
The fact of settling in a place
and being in the same place

00:43:24.100 --> 00:43:26.310
from generation to generation.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:30.520
You even have historical,
I'm talking about fiction,

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:33.840
historical examples of kingdoms

00:43:34.884 --> 00:43:37.513
that were moving kingdoms.

00:43:40.970 --> 00:43:44.380
Kingdoms that were not
limited to one territory.

00:43:44.380 --> 00:43:46.793
They were constantly in motion.

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:51.870
The same thing with trade caravans.

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:54.330
When the concept of a caravan

00:43:54.330 --> 00:43:58.590
applied not only to trade but
also to political formations,

00:43:58.590 --> 00:44:03.590
things were on motion
was enormous if you will.

00:44:05.010 --> 00:44:06.013
That was the law.

00:44:07.730 --> 00:44:10.453
And once again, these
are historical examples.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.060
In West Africa, in Central Africa,

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:18.120
present-day Congo, Angola, all of that,

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:21.220
kingdoms moving from the Atlantic Coast

00:44:21.220 --> 00:44:26.220
towards the Indian Ocean
and changing in the context,

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:30.673
bringing on board new people,

00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:35.860
capturing new religions,

00:44:35.860 --> 00:44:40.860
instituting new social
relations and aesthetic forms.

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:44.520
So that was there.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.523
Now when we talk about
mobility and colonialism,

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:52.763
the colonial logic in that context,

00:44:54.290 --> 00:44:55.853
these how it operates.

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:04.053
First of all, it operates
on the basis of settlement.

00:45:07.130 --> 00:45:11.183
Settlement along the coast to begin with.

00:45:12.830 --> 00:45:17.240
Entrepose, that the combination
of warfare and trade

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:21.180
and missionizing Christianity.

00:45:21.180 --> 00:45:22.303
These are the three.

00:45:25.090 --> 00:45:30.090
So it operates on the basis
of partition of territory

00:45:30.310 --> 00:45:31.960
that is first of all imagined

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:34.963
because it has no borders as such.

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:40.850
It's progressively that
borders are instituted

00:45:40.850 --> 00:45:44.710
in negotiations with local inhabitants

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:48.860
but mostly in negotiation
with other European powers

00:45:48.860 --> 00:45:52.290
all of which culminates
with the Berlin Conference

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:55.123
which I referred to, first that.

00:45:55.960 --> 00:45:59.306
So it's a patchy thing,
absolutely open-ended

00:45:59.306 --> 00:46:02.100
and messy, has no rationale.

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:06.580
You have borders, I mean, you take a rule

00:46:06.580 --> 00:46:08.700
and basically you trace a line.

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:11.150
It doesn't matter what is there.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:12.570
It's a big line.

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:14.010
If you have a map of Africa,

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:17.490
look at for instance the border
between Algeria and Mali.

00:46:17.490 --> 00:46:19.180
It's just like this.

00:46:19.180 --> 00:46:22.370
It cuts through a pre-existing territory

00:46:22.370 --> 00:46:26.033
that was totally fluid because
there was the Sahara Desert.

00:46:27.600 --> 00:46:30.296
Totally nomadic people seek little of,

00:46:30.296 --> 00:46:32.290
and that boom, you put a rule.

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:35.963
Same thing with the border
between Angola and Botswana.

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:39.563
A straightforward line.

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:43.580
No consideration of anything.

00:46:43.580 --> 00:46:46.540
People, mountains, rivers.

00:46:46.540 --> 00:46:51.540
Some borders consider
rivers and physical markers

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:54.130
but others don't.

00:46:54.130 --> 00:46:58.700
So that is what happens with colonialism.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:03.700
And then when the colonial
state is properly instituted

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:07.800
after conquest and occupation

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:15.000
and when it's operating,

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:18.830
it needs them to regulate
people's movements

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:21.333
and the movement of goods.

00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:24.863
The movement of goods is unidirectional.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:29.430
You have the hinterland
you have the coast.

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:32.570
Anything produced in the hinterland

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:36.900
has to go through the
course and shipped outside.

00:47:36.900 --> 00:47:40.600
Extractive economy we have today

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:42.610
which is at the basis therefore

00:47:42.610 --> 00:47:44.908
of the regulation of movements.

00:47:44.908 --> 00:47:47.270
So you have posts where let's see here,

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:51.630
they specialized in the
production of cotton,

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:54.840
over there, production of palm oil

00:47:54.840 --> 00:47:59.753
and you need labor to be
working in these units.

00:48:00.770 --> 00:48:03.760
And the biggest problem of colonialism

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:06.723
in the continent was the problem of labor.

00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:13.260
Because they didn't seem
to ever be enough laborers.

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:16.130
I mean South Africa, that
is absolutely the case.

00:48:16.130 --> 00:48:18.710
They have to go outside of South Africa,

00:48:18.710 --> 00:48:23.710
Mozambique, in Malawi, in places
as far as Zambia, Zimbabwe,

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:28.400
Southern Congo to bring labor

00:48:28.400 --> 00:48:31.183
to work in the mines in the Witwatersrand.

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:37.490
And in so doing, you create
original constellation

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:40.920
with regulated movements because
these are seasonal workers,

00:48:40.920 --> 00:48:42.400
they come and go.

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:47.010
Borders don't really matter that much

00:48:47.010 --> 00:48:49.223
in the case of Southern Africa.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:51.820
They matter and they don't.

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:56.820
They matter because of
the politics of states

00:48:57.860 --> 00:49:01.960
but they don't because the economic demand

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:04.310
requires that they be open.

00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:06.150
So the business of the current state

00:49:06.150 --> 00:49:10.300
is to simultaneously institute borders

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:15.300
but render them flexible
enough to make it possible

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:19.340
for the process of
accumulation to keep going on.

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:21.960
So what is it that,

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:24.570
I want to say in relation
to your question,

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:28.370
I think that we have to
do a proper case study,

00:49:28.370 --> 00:49:33.370
kind of exercise in order to
elicit the many different ways

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:39.570
in which colonialism makes,
remakes and makes the borders.

00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:44.720
So inherit all of that.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:49.650
And in fact, I believe that
people within the continent

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:53.040
were able to move more
freely under colonialism

00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:56.270
than then after colonialism.

00:49:56.270 --> 00:50:00.050
What we see after colonialism
is a crystallization

00:50:00.050 --> 00:50:01.423
of state borders.

00:50:05.030 --> 00:50:08.793
The emergence of the
border becomes a rent.

00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:14.680
It becomes a rent for those social classes

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:16.280
that control the state.

00:50:16.280 --> 00:50:19.110
They use it to extract rents

00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:21.960
which was not the case under colonialism,

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:24.160
which aggravates,

00:50:24.160 --> 00:50:27.260
let's say diminishes
Africans possibilities

00:50:27.260 --> 00:50:30.570
of moving freely within
their own continent.

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:33.880
So it's a long response but it just shows

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:37.880
the extent to which I think
when we say colonialism,

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:39.420
there are many different--

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:41.090
- Could I add to that question?

00:50:41.090 --> 00:50:43.000
And then we'll throw it out.

00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:46.660
It's just your earlier discussion
of speed and acceleration

00:50:46.660 --> 00:50:49.560
because it might seem that actually,

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:53.870
acceleration and speed
makes location and space

00:50:53.870 --> 00:50:55.290
much more important.

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:58.400
So that the response to the speed at light

00:50:58.400 --> 00:50:59.940
of algorithmic trading

00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:01.990
is that you actually have to be located

00:51:01.990 --> 00:51:06.400
even closer to Wall Street
because of the microseconds that,

00:51:07.460 --> 00:51:10.500
the time that it takes
to be engaged in that.

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:15.500
And in many ways that the acceleration

00:51:15.530 --> 00:51:17.600
that you were talking about in speed

00:51:17.600 --> 00:51:21.420
means that one has to be
more tightly connected

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.930
to the infrastructure of speed,

00:51:23.930 --> 00:51:27.260
whether that is airports or data lines

00:51:27.260 --> 00:51:29.310
or interstates or whatever

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:32.110
that makes it much more
difficult for people

00:51:32.110 --> 00:51:35.460
who are what you called go slow

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:39.493
but are actually perhaps
walking at a normal human pace.

00:51:40.800 --> 00:51:42.480
- I would add to that.

00:51:42.480 --> 00:51:45.230
Not just speed but perhaps scale.

00:51:45.230 --> 00:51:50.230
So the potentiality of algorithmic trading

00:51:50.260 --> 00:51:54.080
is its ability to capture
vast amounts of money

00:51:54.080 --> 00:51:55.440
by scaling out.

00:51:55.440 --> 00:51:59.380
By just pennies and fractions
but they're so large

00:51:59.380 --> 00:52:02.074
in volume and scale that they become,

00:52:02.074 --> 00:52:06.920
so the scalar advancement
it seems to me are critical

00:52:06.920 --> 00:52:07.933
in this as well.

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:11.100
- And the connection was simply

00:52:11.100 --> 00:52:14.780
under your own explanation
of how the infrastructure

00:52:14.780 --> 00:52:18.030
of the ports which at one
sense was an acceleration

00:52:18.030 --> 00:52:21.370
and a speeding up of moving
commodities around the world

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:22.710
actually ended up

00:52:24.970 --> 00:52:28.273
prohibiting earlier forms of mobility.

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:31.968
- Yeah, I agree with that.

00:52:31.968 --> 00:52:33.801
- I think we're ready.

00:52:42.520 --> 00:52:44.950
- [Mary Evelyn] Thank you very much.

00:52:44.950 --> 00:52:47.380
My name is Mary Evelyn Tucker
at the environment school.

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:49.790
I'm sorry, I couldn't
come to the other lectures

00:52:49.790 --> 00:52:50.623
'cause of teaching.

00:52:50.623 --> 00:52:55.623
So this question would relate to migration

00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:59.790
due to climate change
or environmental issues

00:52:59.790 --> 00:53:03.070
both in North Africa and
now what we're seeing

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:07.520
in South Africa because of
water depletion and so on.

00:53:07.520 --> 00:53:11.670
So I wonder if, you might
have touched onto this already

00:53:11.670 --> 00:53:14.480
but I'd love to have your comments on this

00:53:14.480 --> 00:53:16.580
because there's 19 million refugees

00:53:16.580 --> 00:53:20.400
for climate change alone
now around the world.

00:53:20.400 --> 00:53:24.290
But clearly, the African
continent has been particularly

00:53:24.290 --> 00:53:27.823
severely hit by drought and other issues.

00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:37.293
- It's a combination of
many, many different factors.

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:43.530
Some of them are ecological
and environmental

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:49.800
but some of these ecological
or environmental factors

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:55.353
themselves the product of
human-induced catastrophes.

00:53:58.560 --> 00:54:03.560
For instance, the wars
in the Great Lakes region

00:54:04.640 --> 00:54:08.830
which comprises the Congo,
Rwanda, Burundi, Uganda,

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:17.170
according to the UN have
caused millions of victims

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:21.730
over a very short period
of time when you add

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:23.530
the genocide in Rwanda

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:27.620
and all that has been going on,

00:54:27.620 --> 00:54:31.050
the wars of predation
in the Eastern Congo,

00:54:31.050 --> 00:54:32.363
a few million people.

00:54:33.830 --> 00:54:38.830
But they have also caused serious
environmental consequences

00:54:40.260 --> 00:54:42.320
which were not there before the war.

00:54:42.320 --> 00:54:47.320
So the war produces ecological
catastrophes which in turn

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:55.440
result in further, further wars.

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:58.520
Most of them was of extraction cobalt.

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:02.710
I mean when every time
you open your computer,

00:55:02.710 --> 00:55:05.840
a lot of your computer
parts of your computer,

00:55:05.840 --> 00:55:08.340
they are coming from there.

00:55:08.340 --> 00:55:09.840
They are coming from the Kumu.

00:55:13.700 --> 00:55:18.227
Your computer to a large
extent is made up of wealth,

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:23.958
mineral wealth extracted from the Congo

00:55:23.958 --> 00:55:28.041
as a result of the
militarization of the economy.

00:55:31.207 --> 00:55:34.490
Militarization,
paramilitarization of the economy.

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:36.260
The two combined.

00:55:36.260 --> 00:55:41.110
So the extractive part
and the abstract part

00:55:41.110 --> 00:55:45.650
all together linked in a
continuum we do not study

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:48.270
because we study economics of extraction

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:53.270
as if they were just located there

00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:58.390
and the financialization
as part of Wall Street

00:55:59.174 --> 00:56:04.063
but there is a link between
Wall Street and Eastern Congo.

00:56:05.743 --> 00:56:10.110
So people migrate for
all kinds of reasons.

00:56:10.110 --> 00:56:11.763
I mean in Johannesburg,

00:56:12.671 --> 00:56:16.270
I have been living in
Johannesburg for the last 17 years

00:56:16.270 --> 00:56:18.290
and we see the city changing.

00:56:18.290 --> 00:56:22.543
Entire neighborhoods, people
only speak in Lingala,

00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:26.963
some other languages from
the rest of the continent.

00:56:27.900 --> 00:56:29.310
These are people who,

00:56:29.310 --> 00:56:30.950
some of them are running away

00:56:30.950 --> 00:56:34.620
from terrible political systems.

00:56:34.620 --> 00:56:38.660
Others are coming because
they want to make a living.

00:56:38.660 --> 00:56:42.710
They will live better,
they will earn little more.

00:56:42.710 --> 00:56:46.733
They will send money to the
places where they come from.

00:56:47.860 --> 00:56:50.373
So the reasons are many.

00:57:01.010 --> 00:57:02.163
They are busy, I mean.

00:57:03.300 --> 00:57:05.040
It's a serious matter, of course.

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:08.989
The question of water and water politics

00:57:08.989 --> 00:57:11.263
is a geopolitical question.

00:57:12.890 --> 00:57:16.373
It's in South Africa.

00:57:18.190 --> 00:57:22.310
It is the result of long years of drought,

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:26.630
it's also the result of,
I would say bad planning.

00:57:26.630 --> 00:57:31.400
We know very well that big, big issue

00:57:31.400 --> 00:57:34.723
for parts of South Africa is water.

00:57:35.920 --> 00:57:38.713
They have been attempts at directing,

00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:43.120
capturing parts of the water in the Congo

00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:45.650
and linking it up with Southern Africa.

00:57:45.650 --> 00:57:48.420
It is possible, the
studies have been done.

00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:50.623
There's not enough
political will to do so.

00:57:51.810 --> 00:57:54.090
So it's all of that.

00:57:54.090 --> 00:57:56.520
But the ecological question clearly

00:57:57.630 --> 00:57:59.570
is in some parts of the world

00:57:59.570 --> 00:58:03.950
at the heart of process of migration.

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:05.050
It's not the only one.

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:09.410
- [Attendee] So thank
you and thank you all

00:58:09.410 --> 00:58:13.230
for your kind references
to my thing on this work.

00:58:13.230 --> 00:58:14.840
I wanna say again thank you.

00:58:14.840 --> 00:58:18.730
For me the these lectures
have been extraordinary

00:58:18.730 --> 00:58:22.190
invitations to expand our imaginary,

00:58:22.190 --> 00:58:25.270
the political imaginary,
the ethical imaginary.

00:58:25.270 --> 00:58:29.690
And so I wanted to ask you to perhaps in,

00:58:29.690 --> 00:58:33.490
I found your invocation of the
Dogon and the network systems

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:37.110
and the debt systems very illuminating

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:41.040
for showing us other
forms of social relations

00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:45.990
tied to land and kinship and
membership to this place.

00:58:45.990 --> 00:58:50.990
And I wondered how that might
be placed in conversation

00:58:51.670 --> 00:58:54.243
with the traditions of African socialism.

00:58:55.290 --> 00:59:00.290
So as to think about another
piece of the hostility

00:59:00.450 --> 00:59:04.870
and racism imbued by
colonialism, not only in Africa

00:59:04.870 --> 00:59:09.570
but the Americas, in Australia

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:11.840
against the Aboriginal populations

00:59:11.840 --> 00:59:16.580
that mobile people were
represented as more primitive

00:59:16.580 --> 00:59:19.180
not only because they would not conform

00:59:19.180 --> 00:59:23.820
to the sedentary order the
imperative of being sedentary

00:59:23.820 --> 00:59:26.620
but because they seemed to be people

00:59:26.620 --> 00:59:31.310
who could not be productive users of land

00:59:31.310 --> 00:59:33.390
because they did not accumulate.

00:59:33.390 --> 00:59:36.450
And the imperative in
the post-colonial era

00:59:36.450 --> 00:59:39.220
of as you've written about beautifully

00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:42.860
is a new administrative
control of the economies

00:59:42.860 --> 00:59:46.700
of the former colonized nations

00:59:46.700 --> 00:59:50.760
by insisting and demanding certain modes

00:59:50.760 --> 00:59:53.740
of production accumulation.

00:59:53.740 --> 00:59:57.640
So I just I wanted to ask you
if there's a way to connect

00:59:58.530 --> 01:00:02.370
the vision that you gave
us around the pre-colonial

01:00:02.370 --> 01:00:07.370
social worlds and forms of
habitation to another vision

01:00:07.410 --> 01:00:12.010
of a African socialism which
is also rich tradition.

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:12.843
Thank you.

01:00:22.301 --> 01:00:23.134
- Okay.

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:29.970
There have been historical
experience of socialism

01:00:30.980 --> 01:00:35.980
in the continent since
the end of colonialism.

01:00:39.180 --> 01:00:44.180
Different kinds of
experiments in Guinea Conakry,

01:00:49.150 --> 01:00:51.423
in Angola, in Mozambique.

01:00:54.700 --> 01:00:55.643
Even in Senegal.

01:00:57.960 --> 01:01:00.860
Senghor for instance was a major

01:01:02.350 --> 01:01:04.423
theorist of African socialism.

01:01:06.692 --> 01:01:08.193
And of course in Tanzania.

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:12.850
Tanzania was the most advanced experiment

01:01:12.850 --> 01:01:16.883
in that sense with Nyerere
and his concept of Ujamaa.

01:01:19.290 --> 01:01:23.373
Most of those experiments
have failed, quote, unquote.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:29.440
They have failed to extend that.

01:01:29.440 --> 01:01:32.840
Today, you hardly find anybody,

01:01:32.840 --> 01:01:37.783
even doing research on
so-called African socialism.

01:01:44.880 --> 01:01:49.880
Most of these from say, theoretical

01:01:50.030 --> 01:01:51.753
ideological point of view,

01:01:53.530 --> 01:01:57.200
most of these experiments

01:01:59.640 --> 01:02:02.363
were aiming at domesticating,

01:02:03.340 --> 01:02:05.110
let's say Marx, basically.

01:02:05.110 --> 01:02:10.110
There were part of a project
of domestication of Marx

01:02:10.760 --> 01:02:13.723
in the conditions of Africa.

01:02:15.370 --> 01:02:19.640
And the overall premise

01:02:19.640 --> 01:02:24.210
was that these African
societies were governed

01:02:27.890 --> 01:02:31.643
by the law of community.

01:02:33.360 --> 01:02:38.080
Distinction between what is
communal and what is individual.

01:02:38.080 --> 01:02:40.550
And that property relations

01:02:40.550 --> 01:02:45.550
were hardly privatized.

01:02:46.060 --> 01:02:47.810
Property relations were communal,

01:02:47.810 --> 01:02:52.433
land belonged to a community
rather than to individuals.

01:02:55.440 --> 01:03:00.040
There was an element of this that was true

01:03:00.040 --> 01:03:04.583
and another element that
was highly mythical.

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:08.510
But the politics,

01:03:08.510 --> 01:03:13.420
it's about truth, it's also about myth.

01:03:13.420 --> 01:03:14.620
Nothing wrong with that.

01:03:17.084 --> 01:03:20.167
Now, you are asking me to make a link

01:03:23.800 --> 01:03:26.180
between, or as seriously

01:03:27.252 --> 01:03:30.050
about African pre-colonial geometrics

01:03:31.625 --> 01:03:34.393
and the idea of African socialism.

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:38.933
I don't think there was
a direct connection.

01:03:40.850 --> 01:03:43.373
Talking about these
historical experiments.

01:03:44.509 --> 01:03:47.853
There was hardly any connection.

01:03:50.600 --> 01:03:51.570
There was

01:03:53.950 --> 01:03:57.527
an exercise of reinvention of traditions

01:04:01.010 --> 01:04:05.090
or some elements of so-called traditions

01:04:05.090 --> 01:04:08.433
for purposes of political efficacy.

01:04:11.622 --> 01:04:12.455
Otherwise,

01:04:17.800 --> 01:04:20.160
what I was trying to say

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:23.830
was that if we look at the different,

01:04:23.830 --> 01:04:25.693
call them archives of the world.

01:04:27.900 --> 01:04:30.510
Something we could call
the African archive

01:04:31.628 --> 01:04:32.673
to be defined on.

01:04:33.700 --> 01:04:37.627
If we look at the different
archives of the world,

01:04:39.730 --> 01:04:42.753
this allows us to imagine.

01:04:48.340 --> 01:04:53.340
Let's say different paradigms
of almost everything

01:04:53.880 --> 01:04:57.600
of how gender relations can be organized,

01:04:57.600 --> 01:05:00.813
how it is the property
relations are organized,

01:05:01.660 --> 01:05:05.343
what types of relations
people have with the divine,

01:05:06.470 --> 01:05:08.160
religious field.

01:05:08.160 --> 01:05:11.080
There's a multiplicity of paradigms

01:05:12.330 --> 01:05:14.550
if we think in terms of the planetary

01:05:14.550 --> 01:05:17.660
in terms of the humanity as a whole

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:21.670
which are not reducible to those paradigms

01:05:21.670 --> 01:05:24.270
that end up dominating our world,

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:26.880
mostly Western paradigm.

01:05:26.880 --> 01:05:30.933
Paradigms coming from one
form of human experience.

01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:34.893
So the call was to,

01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:40.260
in this age when we are so interconnected,

01:05:40.260 --> 01:05:44.363
we cannot produce knowledge
that is simply provincial.

01:05:45.500 --> 01:05:50.500
What our world requires
now are forms of knowledge

01:05:50.507 --> 01:05:55.210
that are as much as possible

01:05:56.730 --> 01:05:59.920
a manifestation of the multiplicities

01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:01.823
of the archives we inherit.

01:06:02.960 --> 01:06:07.960
So if the debate is about
how to govern mobility

01:06:08.060 --> 01:06:11.250
in this century, which by the way,

01:06:11.250 --> 01:06:13.053
by the end of this century,

01:06:14.180 --> 01:06:16.740
the biggest number of people in our world

01:06:16.740 --> 01:06:20.153
will be coming from the
continent of Africa.

01:06:21.710 --> 01:06:23.743
By 2050,

01:06:24.970 --> 01:06:29.920
Africa will have recovered
the demographic gap

01:06:30.790 --> 01:06:35.103
it suffered during the
Atlantic slave trade.

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:38.423
And by the end of the century,

01:06:39.470 --> 01:06:43.320
most living beings, human beings on Earth

01:06:43.320 --> 01:06:45.273
will be found in the continent.

01:06:47.580 --> 01:06:51.430
Nigeria will be bigger,
demographically speaking

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:54.013
than the United States of America.

01:06:56.970 --> 01:07:01.940
The youngest people on Earth
will be coming from there.

01:07:01.940 --> 01:07:06.760
We'll have a kind of map
of the world population

01:07:06.760 --> 01:07:11.760
in which the oldest people and
the smaller number of people

01:07:12.020 --> 01:07:14.690
will be located in places such as here,

01:07:14.690 --> 01:07:18.770
Europe, Japan, of course,
partly Latin America

01:07:19.620 --> 01:07:22.250
and the biggest number of youngest people

01:07:22.250 --> 01:07:26.970
will be coming from Asia
and particularly Africa.

01:07:26.970 --> 01:07:31.760
So the question of who owns the Earth?

01:07:31.760 --> 01:07:33.150
How do we divide it?

01:07:33.150 --> 01:07:37.690
How do we apportion it
will be resolved partly

01:07:37.690 --> 01:07:41.310
through the demographic calculus.

01:07:41.310 --> 01:07:45.233
So demography will become geopolitics.

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:49.733
It's all there is.

01:07:51.620 --> 01:07:56.620
And naturally, what we
will see is a process

01:07:57.350 --> 01:07:58.860
which is already going on.

01:07:58.860 --> 01:08:02.530
We call it migration crisis, whatever.

01:08:02.530 --> 01:08:04.130
That's the wrong term.

01:08:04.130 --> 01:08:07.750
What is going on is a process of,

01:08:07.750 --> 01:08:09.950
I don't know the term in English,

01:08:09.950 --> 01:08:11.277
in French it is (speaking
in foreign language).

01:08:13.387 --> 01:08:14.320
(speaking in foreign language)

01:08:14.320 --> 01:08:18.970
Is the process of, it's
like under colonialism.

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:22.120
Colonialism was a form of
(speaking in foreign language)

01:08:22.120 --> 01:08:23.220
of the world.

01:08:23.220 --> 01:08:27.063
People go into Australia, South
Africa, Asia and all that.

01:08:28.530 --> 01:08:31.603
It won't happen like that
by the end of this century.

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:37.680
It won't happen like that
because we are beyond colonialism

01:08:40.100 --> 01:08:42.150
unless we go and colonize another planet.

01:08:43.920 --> 01:08:46.720
But it will happen
through population move,

01:08:46.720 --> 01:08:48.310
people moving.

01:08:48.310 --> 01:08:52.510
The key is it will be
unlocked by people moving

01:08:52.510 --> 01:08:54.033
in spite of borders.

01:08:55.820 --> 01:08:59.940
That's how the Earth will
be re-peopled if you want.

01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:02.170
I don't know what the term
(speaking in foreign language)

01:09:02.170 --> 01:09:03.150
means in English.

01:09:03.150 --> 01:09:06.590
You will find it but that's
what I'm talking about.

01:09:06.590 --> 01:09:11.590
So, now if you want to
understand, therefore,

01:09:12.460 --> 01:09:14.990
that process it seems
to me we have to open

01:09:14.990 --> 01:09:17.230
as many archives as possible.

01:09:17.230 --> 01:09:20.720
So the question of the
government of human mobility

01:09:20.720 --> 01:09:25.610
in this century cannot
be resolved exclusively

01:09:25.610 --> 01:09:30.610
by looking into the anti-humanist
archive of the West.

01:09:32.670 --> 01:09:37.250
Because when it comes to
borders, people's movements,

01:09:37.250 --> 01:09:39.760
the Western archive is anti-human.

01:09:39.760 --> 01:09:42.883
It is pro-capital, pro-commodity.

01:09:43.930 --> 01:09:45.920
The question of the fourth freedom,

01:09:45.920 --> 01:09:49.173
it privileges things over people.

01:09:50.150 --> 01:09:53.280
In the continent, if
we look at the archive

01:09:53.280 --> 01:09:54.253
of the continent,

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:58.060
people and things are interwoven.

01:09:58.060 --> 01:10:01.450
You cannot allow things to move freely

01:10:01.450 --> 01:10:03.863
without allowing people to go with them.

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:08.290
Because if you separate
people from things,

01:10:08.290 --> 01:10:11.530
you destroy both people and things.

01:10:11.530 --> 01:10:15.550
You make the Earth inhabitable.

01:10:15.550 --> 01:10:17.660
So the question of the 21st century

01:10:17.660 --> 01:10:22.353
is that all question
of the right to abode,

01:10:23.720 --> 01:10:28.600
the right to inhabit, the
entirety of the planet

01:10:28.600 --> 01:10:32.933
and return when as we
see almost everywhere.

01:10:34.020 --> 01:10:38.650
Many abodes are being
rendered and inhabitable

01:10:38.650 --> 01:10:39.670
for those who are there.

01:10:39.670 --> 01:10:42.090
That's why they want to leave.

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:46.103
They want to leave because
the abode has been rendered

01:10:46.103 --> 01:10:48.230
an inhabitable.

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:49.780
So the question is how do we

01:10:51.630 --> 01:10:55.520
recover some sense of habitability

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:58.540
whereas I was arguing earlier on,

01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:01.630
I don't have to justify who I am

01:11:01.630 --> 01:11:03.713
and why I look the way I look.

01:11:04.560 --> 01:11:07.313
It entails the end of classification.

01:11:08.210 --> 01:11:12.360
The end of classification
as the opening of the world

01:11:12.360 --> 01:11:15.813
to all who inhabit it, people and things.

01:11:17.310 --> 01:11:20.060
So that's what, I mean,
the whole exercise,

01:11:20.060 --> 01:11:21.393
that is what it was.

01:11:24.580 --> 01:11:28.390
- [Moderator] I want to say
that we do have more opportunity

01:11:28.390 --> 01:11:31.200
for conversation in the reception to come

01:11:31.200 --> 01:11:33.800
and it seems to me that that extraordinary

01:11:33.800 --> 01:11:35.120
summarizing statement,

01:11:35.120 --> 01:11:37.265
an extraordinary summarizing statement.

01:11:37.265 --> 01:11:41.970
Not a utopian one but
nonetheless, a futuristic one

01:11:41.970 --> 01:11:46.910
and one that shows us
the amazing challenges

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:50.160
to humanity as a whole
that you are posing to us.

01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:51.260
That has to be a place

01:11:51.260 --> 01:11:53.760
where we stop this part of
the conversation at least

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:54.820
and move on.

01:11:54.820 --> 01:11:57.717
So thank you so much to our
main speaker, Achille Mbembe

01:11:57.717 --> 01:11:59.420
and to our panelists, Inderpal and Mike.

01:12:00.647 --> 01:12:02.234
- Thank you.

01:12:02.234 --> 01:12:03.317
Thanks, Mike.

01:12:03.317 --> 01:12:06.650
(attendees applauding)

